\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align left
We've all seen a high order detonation,
 it is a chemical chain reaction that starts at one point and expands outwards in all directions as the chemical energy in molecular bonds breaks down and causes a shockwave radiating outward.
 Low order detonation is a gunpowder explosion,
 the high order works faster and generates orders of magnitude more force.
 Both produce gas expansion,
 but a high order explosion produces orders of magnitude more gas expansion as above.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename ExplosionRing.png
	lyxscale 30
	width 10cm

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An explosion,
 from low order detonation to a high order detonation,
 all begin at a detonation point at wherever the chain reaction starts.
 Even a deflagration,
 which is when that same chemical starts burning at subsonic speeds instead of supersonic due to the design,
 shape,
 and timing of the detonation,
 still starts from a point near the detonator (a sensitized less powerful explosive that kickstarts the explosion chain reaction) and expands out like the above image.
 Concentric rings from a central point are the same shockwave advancing in all directions is an omnidirectional explosion.
 The more gas expands,
 the more energy is converted from a chemical by shockwave overpassing molecules that detonate.
 Chain reaction accelerates gases nearby as the chain reaction advances.
 When you contain the explosive in a steel container like the casing of a bomb or missile or artillery shell,
 those successive gas expansions build up stress on the container,
 transferring energy into structural deformation,
 and then the container bursts and sends energy outward with more force through the material storing potential energy from the blast.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Why was Charlie Kirk explosion not omnidirectional?
 If you looked at an omnidirectional explosion,
 not by a shaped charge,
